Binary Search: Best / Average / Worst Case Analysis :-

## Problem Statement :-
Understanding the best, worst and average case complexitiies of Binary search.

## Approach :-
- Test array: `arr[i] = 2*i` (sirf even numbers), isse odd numbers array me **nahi** hote (unsuccessful search test ho paata hai).
- **Best case:** middle element search karo.
- **Average case:** array ke saare present keys search karo, comparisons ka average lo.
- **Worst case:** saare absent keys me se maximum comparisons lo.
- 1 comparison = 1 loop iteration (ek `mid` check).
- Results `case_analysis.csv` me save hote hain (graph ke liye).

## Pseudocode :-

ANALYZE(n):
    arr[i] = 2*i  for i in 0..n-1
    best    = comparisons(arr, arr[(n-1)/2])
    average = (sum of comparisons(arr, arr[i]) for all i) / n
    worst   = max of comparisons(arr, k) for all absent k
    theory  = floor(log2 n) + 1
    print n, best, average, worst, theory


## Flowchart :-

flowchart TD
    A([Start]) --> B[For each n in sizes]
    B --> C[Build array arr i = 2i]
    C --> D[Best: search middle element]
    D --> E[Average: search all present keys, take mean]
    E --> F[Worst: search all absent keys, take max]
    F --> G[Compare with floor log2 n + 1]
    G --> H[Print row + write CSV]
    H --> I{More sizes?}
    I -- Yes --> B
    I -- No --> Z([End])


## Time & Space Complexity (Theory)
| Case | Kab hota hai | Comparisons | Time |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Best** | Key pehli baar me hi middle pe mil jaye | 1 | **O(1)** |
| **Average** | Key random jagah ho | ~ log2(n) - 1 | **O(log n)** |
| **Worst** | Key last level pe mile ya hai hi nahi | floor(log2 n) + 1 | **O(log n)** |

**Space:** O(1) iterative, O(log n) recursive.
Worst case derivation: `T(n) = T(n/2) + 1`, `T(1) = 1` -> `T(n) = floor(log2 n) + 1`.

## Sample Input / Output :-              
n         Best    Average     Worst   floor(log2 n)+1

10        1       2.90        4       4
100       1       5.80        7       7

